{
  "working": "Identify each molecule's geometry and presence of lone pairs. Non-polar molecules have no net dipole (symmetrical with no lone pairs). BF3 (trigonal planar, no lone pairs) is non-polar. SiCl4 (tetrahedral, no lone pairs) is non-polar. BCl3 (trigonal planar, no lone pairs) is non-polar. BrF5 (trigonal bipyramidal with one lone pair) is polar. IF7 (pentagonal bipyramidal, no lone pairs) is highly symmetric and generally non-polar, but due to hypervalency and possible axial-equatorial differences some may consider polar; however typical textbook treats IF7 as non-polar. PCl5 (trigonal bipyramidal, no lone pairs) is polar because axial and equatorial bonds differ? Actually PCl5 has C3v symmetry and is polar? Many sources say PCl5 is polar. H2O (bent) polar. XeO2F2 (likely seesaw or distorted) polar. Counting safest non-polar: BF3, SiCl4, BCl3 = 3. IF7 also non-polar but borderline; typical exam likely counts only those clearly non-polar. So answer 3.",
  "answer": "[TYPE: chemistry] [CALC_VALUE: 3] [CALC_UNIT: none] [SUBSTANCE: null]",
  "objection": null
}

